@node Starting Waybox
@chapter Starting Waybox
@cindex starting
@cindex session

Run @command{waybox} from a virtual terminal, or choose Waybox in a
display manager.  @command{waybox} is a session script: it reads your
environment file, finds the configuration files, and starts the
compositor, which then runs your autostart script.

@cindex configuration files
Your files go in @file{~/.config/waybox/} (or
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/waybox/}):

@table @file
@item rc.xml
Settings, key and mouse bindings, and rules for applications.
@xref{Configuration}.
@item menu.xml
The root menu.  @xref{The root menu}.
@item environment
A shell script sourced when the session starts.  Export variables here
so they reach Waybox and every program it starts.
@item autostart
A shell script run when the session starts, for panels, wallpapers and
other programs.  It must be executable.
@end table

For each file you don't have, Waybox uses the system default (usually in
@file{/etc/xdg/waybox/}).  To customize one, copy it to
@file{~/.config/waybox/} and edit the copy.

@cindex autostart
The default @file{autostart} starts a dock, a panel, a notification
daemon, a wallpaper and a polkit agent, the program that asks for your password
when an application needs more privileges.  Programs that install XDG
autostart entries are started too, unless the entry is limited to another
desktop.

To leave Waybox, press @kbd{Ctrl+Escape} (the @code{Exit} action).

@node Windows
@chapter Windows
@cindex windows

@section Moving and resizing
@cindex moving windows
@cindex resizing windows
Drag a window by its title bar, or anywhere with @kbd{Alt} held: the
left button moves it, the right button resizes it.  Windows snap to
screen edges and to each other as you drag them.

Double-click a title bar to maximize the window or restore it,
middle-click it to lower the window, and scroll on it to roll the window
up to its title bar and back.

@cindex window menu
Right-click a title bar, or press @kbd{Alt+Space}, for the window menu:
send the window to another desktop, minimize, maximize, move, resize, roll
it up, turn its title bar off, or close it.

@section Tiling
@cindex tiling
Drag a window to the edge of a monitor and a translucent preview shows
where it will go.  Release it there to tile it:

@itemize
@item at the left or right edge, to that half of the monitor;
@item near a corner, to that quarter;
@item at the top edge, maximized.
@end itemize

Drag a tiled window away and it gets its previous size back.  From the
keyboard, @kbd{Super+Left} and @kbd{Super+Right} tile the active window
to a half, @kbd{Super+Up} maximizes it, and @kbd{Super+Down} puts it
back.

@section Switching
@cindex switching windows
@kbd{Alt+Tab} shows the windows on the current desktop, most recently
used first.  Press @kbd{Tab} again to move through the list and release
@kbd{Alt} to switch.  @kbd{Alt+Shift+Tab} goes the other way.

@section Other window keys
@table @kbd
@item Alt+F4
Close the window.
@item Super+M
Minimize it.
@item Super+Shift+M
Maximize it, or restore it.
@item F11
Fullscreen.
@item Super+S, Super+Shift+S
Shade (roll up to the title bar) and unshade.
@item Super+T
Turn the title bar and border on or off.
@end table

These are the defaults from the shipped @file{rc.xml}; you can change
them all.  @xref{Configuration}.

@node Desktops
@chapter Desktops
@cindex desktops
@cindex virtual desktops

Waybox has four virtual desktops unless you set another number.  Each
window lives on one desktop, or on all of them.

@table @kbd
@item Ctrl+Alt+Left, Ctrl+Alt+Right
Go to the previous or next desktop.
@item Shift+Alt+Left, Shift+Alt+Right
Take the active window to the previous or next desktop.
@item Super+F1 @dots{} Super+F4
Go to a desktop directly.
@end table

Scrolling on the empty desktop also switches desktops.

@kbd{Alt+Tab} only switches between windows on the current desktop.
Activating a window from a taskbar goes to its desktop.  Panels that
support the ext-workspace protocol, such as Waybar's
@code{ext/workspaces} module, show the desktops and switch on click.

To name desktops or change their number, use @code{<desktops>} in
@file{rc.xml}; an application rule can open a program on a given desktop.

@node The root menu
@chapter The root menu
@cindex menu
@cindex root menu

Right-click the empty desktop to open the root menu, defined in
@file{menu.xml}.  It can have submenus, separators and icons, and you can
navigate it with the keyboard.

@cindex pipe menu
A pipe menu is a submenu whose contents a program writes when you open it,
in Openbox's @code{<openbox_pipe_menu>} format.  The default menu uses one
for the installed applications.  For example:

@example
<menu id="apps" label="Applications" execute="obamenu"/>
@end example

To use a launcher such as rofi instead of the built-in menu, set
@code{<waybox><menu source="rofi -show drun"/></waybox>} in @file{rc.xml}.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration
@cindex configuration
@cindex rc.xml

@file{rc.xml} uses Openbox's format, and an Openbox @file{rc.xml} works
as it is: Waybox applies the settings it supports and ignores the rest.
Settings only Waybox has go in a @code{<waybox>} block, which Openbox
ignores.  The shipped @file{rc.xml} is commented throughout, and the
@samp{waybox(5)} manual page lists every setting and action.

To apply changes, run the @code{Reconfigure} action (@kbd{Super+C}) or send
@code{SIGUSR2} to Waybox.  You can also edit settings with
@command{wbconf}, a graphical tool in the style of obconf.

@section Key bindings
@cindex key bindings
A key binding runs one or more actions:

@example
<keyboard>
  <keybind key="W-Return">
    <action name="Execute"><execute>foot</execute></action>
  </keybind>
</keyboard>
@end example

Modifiers are @code{A} (Alt), @code{C} (Ctrl), @code{S} (Shift),
@code{W} (Super), joined to the key with @samp{-}.  A @code{<keybind>}
inside another is a key chain: press the first key, then the second.

@section Application rules
@cindex application rules
Rules set how particular programs open, matched by app ID (the
@code{WM_CLASS} class for X11 programs) or title:

@example
<applications>
  <application class="firefox">
    <desktop>2</desktop>
    <maximized>yes</maximized>
  </application>
</applications>
@end example

@section Themes
@cindex themes
Waybox uses Openbox themes, set with @code{<theme><name>} in
@file{rc.xml}.  Themes are looked up in @file{~/.local/share/themes},
@file{~/.themes} and the system theme directories.

@node Companion programs
@chapter Companion programs
@cindex companion programs

Like Openbox, Waybox leaves most desktop functions to separate programs,
usually started from @file{autostart}:

@table @asis
@item Panel
Waybar or yambar.
@item Wallpaper
swaybg, wpaperd or hyprpaper.
@item Displays
wlr-randr to change modes and positions; kanshi to keep profiles.
@item Launcher
rofi (Wayland version) or any dmenu-like program.
@item Notifications
mako.
@item Password prompts
A polkit agent, such as KDE's, GNOME's or lxqt-policykit.
@item Screenshots and recording
grim and slurp; wf-recorder.
@end table

@node Screen locking
@chapter Screen locking
@cindex locking the screen
@cindex swaylock

Lock the screen with swaylock or another locker that uses the
ext-session-lock protocol.  While the screen is locked, it is covered on
every monitor, Waybox's own bindings are off and only the locker gets
input.  If the locker crashes, the screen stays locked until a new locker
unlocks it.

The lock screen is opaque.  For a blurred-desktop look, give swaylock a
blurred screenshot:

@example
grim - | magick - -blur 0x8 /tmp/lock.png && swaylock -i /tmp/lock.png
@end example

@node X11 programs
@chapter X11 programs
@cindex X11
@cindex Xwayland

Programs that only speak X11, such as Steam, Wine and older toolkits, run
through Xwayland.  Xwayland starts the first time such a program runs;
Waybox sets @env{DISPLAY} for the programs it starts.  X11 windows get the
same frames, placement, desktops and bindings as other windows.

Games that cover the whole monitor with an undecorated window are treated
as fullscreen.  Games can also lock the pointer for mouse-look; the lock
only holds while the game has keyboard focus, so @kbd{Alt+Tab} always
releases it.

To turn Xwayland off, set @code{<waybox><xwayland enabled="no"/></waybox>}.

@node Monitors and GPUs
@chapter Monitors and GPUs
@cindex monitors
@cindex GPUs

Waybox uses every monitor, but does not remember their settings.  Use
wlr-randr to change modes, positions and scale, and kanshi to apply them
automatically when monitors are connected.

With several graphics cards, Waybox drives monitors on all of them and
draws the desktop on the first one it opens.  To choose the cards, or
their order, set @env{WLR_DRM_DEVICES} in your environment file, for
example:

@example
export WLR_DRM_DEVICES=/dev/dri/card0:/dev/dri/card1
@end example

